From 1419b01c1ccfcab6c5b543233812ddb5d798b438 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 07:32:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make every provider dependency survive a bad ten minutes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four failure paths shared one shape: a job the tenant paid for died
because a single vendor was having a bad ten minutes. Each is now the
same failover the image pipeline already runs.

Preflight before funding: a video job's dependencies are checked while
refusing is still free. 400 when a capability has nothing configured,
503 when everything configured has an open circuit breaker. No network
calls — it reads the in-process breaker state real jobs already
maintain, so it never refuses a job that would have worked.

Narration failover: TTS is a provider registry now (built-in OpenAI,
Deepgram Aura as fallback, reusing the Deepgram key the admin already
saved for speech-to-text). Failover is whole-track, because cue timings
require one consistent audio format across the take.

Speech-to-text failover: a voice note the tenant just recorded cannot
be re-recorded, so a transient failure tries up to two other configured
providers, healthiest first.

Video model failover: there is one AI video provider, so the chain walks
that provider's other catalog models for the engine mode — a queue
backed up behind one hosted model is the common failure. A fallback
model this account cannot reach never masks the configured model's error.

Permanent failures (rejected prompt, bad key, unusable audio) fail
immediately everywhere: another candidate would reject them too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
